          <div id="znid-622574448782"
            class="zn-element-wrapper 
                
              " 
            data-type="text"
            
          >
            <div class="zn-element zn-lg  ">
              <div id="" class="zn-text "   ><p>Momma passed away twelve years ago, but in my heart, she came back to life the minute I heard Sierra Hull’s stunning new release “Spitfire:” </p><p><br></p><p>She's a spitfire, spitfire</p><p>Queen of a tip toe high wire</p><p>And you really can't blame her</p><p>When nobody can tame her </p><p>Oh, she's a spitfire</p><p><br></p><p>The song is off of East Tennessee native and Grammy nominee Sierra Hull’s latest album “A Tip Toe High Wire.” It’s about an older woman whose life has been marred by disasters — her first husband dies tragically, then her baby too — but she grows tough over time. I think a lot of Appalachian women have lived and survived that way.</p></div>
            </div>
            
            
          </div>
        

